Beryllium: a renderer gives up privileges, forking, core files and tracing at its lockdown

Purpose: plan/18 section 3.2, P3 (plan/06 section 3.1; feature matrix
  S-HRD-01, S-HRD-03). Capability mode takes away a renderer's access to
  names: paths, addresses, other processes. It leaves the renderer able to
  fork, to gain privileges by executing a set-user-ID file it holds a
  descriptor for, and to be attached to by any process of the same user.
  None of that is something a renderer does, and each is a step in known
  ways out of a sandbox.
  - sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc: HardenRenderer(), called
    after the descriptor sweep and the limits on the standard streams and
    before cap_enter(), when the lockdown is on:
      - PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL: an exec no longer changes credentials;
      - RLIMIT_CORE 0;
      - RLIMIT_NPROC 0: fork fails (the kernel counts against this limit in
        fork1() only; threads come from thr_new(), which does not look at
        it);
      - PROC_TRACE_CTL disabled: no ptrace, no ktrace, no debugging sysctls
        (kern.proc.filedesc, which is what procstat -f reads) and no core
        from this process, for another process of the same user.
    A call that fails ends the lockdown, and so the renderer, with the
    reason in the log: fail closed, as for a descriptor outside the policy.
    The lockdown's log line gains hardening=<what was set>.
  Two features, read only when the lockdown is on:
    BerylliumRendererHardening, on by default: the first three calls. A
      switch so that a run can tell them from the lockdown itself if one
      of them breaks something on a machine nobody has tried.
    BerylliumRendererTraceable, off by default: leaves tracing allowed.
      The test suite's descriptor checks (procstat -f and -C), the Capsicum
      survey (ktrace) and the debugger-driven tests all need to trace a
      renderer, so those runs switch this on, and their reports say that
      the renderer they looked at differed from the default by this call.
  The kernel refuses PROC_TRACE_CTL on a process that is being traced
    already (EBUSY). That is a debugger or ktrace started on the renderer
    before its lockdown; the renderer ends and the message names the switch.
  Not verified on a machine: that RLIMIT_NPROC 0 leaves thread creation
    alone on FreeBSD 14, 15 and 16 (read from kern_fork.c and kern_thr.c;
    the standalone job of plan/18 section 0.2 is to confirm it). If it does
    not, every renderer ends at its first thread and
    --disable-features=BerylliumRendererHardening shows it at once.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.22). The lockdown's own code, taken
  from the patched file, type-checks against FreeBSD 16's headers with
  stand-ins for Chromium's declarations.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py test_lockdown_log
  reads hardening= from each lockdown line and, under the gate on a build
  that writes it, requires no-new-privileges, no-core and no-fork in every
  renderer; class Untraceable starts a browser with the lockdown and
  without BerylliumRendererTraceable and requires that procstat cannot
  read a renderer's descriptors while it still reads its capability mode.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, process confinement)

--- a/sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc
+++ b/sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc
@@ -93,6 +93,26 @@
 // instead of failing quietly.
 BASE_FEATURE(kBerylliumCapsicumTrap, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
 
+// Beryllium (plan/18 section 3.2, P3; plan/06 section 3.1; feature matrix
+// S-HRD-01, S-HRD-03): what a renderer gives up at its lockdown besides
+// ambient authority. Capability mode leaves a process able to fork, to gain
+// privileges by executing a set-user-ID file it holds a descriptor for
+// (fexecve), and to be traced by any process of the same user; none of that
+// is something a renderer does.
+//   kBerylliumRendererHardening, on by default: no new privileges, no core
+//     files, no new processes. A switch so that a run can tell these calls
+//     from the lockdown itself if one of them breaks something.
+//   kBerylliumRendererTraceable, off by default: leaves tracing allowed.
+//     Without it a locked-down renderer cannot be attached to by a debugger,
+//     ktraced, or have its descriptors read through kern.proc.filedesc
+//     (procstat -f) by another process of the same user, which is the point,
+//     and which is also everything the test suite's descriptor checks, the
+//     Capsicum survey and the debugger-driven tests use. Those runs switch
+//     this on and say so.
+// Each applies only when the lockdown itself is on.
+BASE_FEATURE(kBerylliumRendererHardening, base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+BASE_FEATURE(kBerylliumRendererTraceable, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
 // Written with utrace(2) at the lockdown point, whether or not the renderer
 // then locks down: the Capsicum survey (freebsd/tests/browser, ktrace -t u)
 // splits each process's trace there, separating what happens before the
@@ -158,9 +178,67 @@
   int limited = 0;
   int threads = -1;          // threads in the process at the lockdown point
   std::string thread_names;  // their names (ki_tdname), comma-separated
+  std::string hardening;     // what HardenRenderer() did, comma-separated
   std::string violation;     // empty when the policy held
 };
 
+// The calls of kBerylliumRendererHardening and kBerylliumRendererTraceable,
+// made after the descriptor sweep and before cap_enter(). Returns false,
+// with errno set and |violation| saying which, if one of them failed: the
+// lockdown then fails and the renderer ends, as for a descriptor outside
+// the policy. |done| receives what was set, for the lockdown's log line.
+//   - PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL: an exec of a set-user-ID or set-group-ID file
+//     no longer changes credentials.
+//   - RLIMIT_CORE 0: no core file (capability mode already refuses to
+//     create one; this is the limit that says so).
+//   - RLIMIT_NPROC 0: fork fails. The kernel counts processes against this
+//     limit in fork1() only; threads are made by thr_new(), which does not
+//     look at it.
+//   - PROC_TRACE_CTL: no ptrace, no ktrace, no debugging sysctls and no core
+//     from this process. The kernel refuses to set it on a process that is
+//     being traced already (EBUSY), which is a debugger or ktrace started on
+//     the renderer before its lockdown: the renderer ends, and the message
+//     names the switch a test run needs.
+bool HardenRenderer(std::string* done, std::string* violation) {
+  if (base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kBerylliumRendererHardening)) {
+#if defined(PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL)
+    int no_new_privs = PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_ENABLE;
+    if (procctl(P_PID, getpid(), PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL, &no_new_privs) != 0) {
+      *violation = "PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL was refused";
+      return false;
+    }
+    *done += "no-new-privileges,";
+#endif
+    const struct rlimit none = {0, 0};
+    if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_CORE, &none) != 0) {
+      *violation = "RLIMIT_CORE was not lowered";
+      return false;
+    }
+    *done += "no-core,";
+    if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_NPROC, &none) != 0) {
+      *violation = "RLIMIT_NPROC was not lowered";
+      return false;
+    }
+    *done += "no-fork,";
+  }
+  if (base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kBerylliumRendererTraceable)) {
+    *done += "traceable";
+    return true;
+  }
+  int trace = PROC_TRACE_CTL_DISABLE;
+  if (procctl(P_PID, getpid(), PROC_TRACE_CTL, &trace) != 0) {
+    *violation =
+        errno == EBUSY
+            ? "tracing was not disabled, because this process is being "
+              "traced already (a debugger, or ktrace); a run that traces "
+              "renderers needs --enable-features=BerylliumRendererTraceable"
+            : "PROC_TRACE_CTL was refused";
+    return false;
+  }
+  *done += "untraceable";
+  return true;
+}
+
 // The number of threads in this process, from the kernel, or -1 with the
 // reason in the log. kern.proc.pid with KERN_PROC_INC_THREAD answers one
 // kinfo_proc per thread, each starting with its own size (ki_structsize),
@@ -334,6 +412,9 @@
     confinement->violation = "the standard streams could not be limited";
     return false;
   }
+  if (!HardenRenderer(&confinement->hardening, &confinement->violation)) {
+    return false;
+  }
   utrace(kLockdownMarker, sizeof(kLockdownMarker) - 1);
   if (cap_enter() != 0) {
     return false;
@@ -480,7 +561,10 @@
                    << " threads=" << confinement.threads << " thread_names="
                    << (confinement.thread_names.empty()
                            ? "-"
-                           : confinement.thread_names);
+                           : confinement.thread_names)
+                   << " hardening="
+                   << (confinement.hardening.empty() ? "-"
+                                                     : confinement.hardening);
     }
   }
 
